{"items":[{"targetId":"n0000079","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"A lower-FLOP parameterization is useful only if its reduced computational cost does not materially worsen the optimization or approximation behavior of the resulting mixing matrices.","densityScore":0.71,"relation":"supports","reason":"Makes the cost-versus-quality comparison decision-relevant."}]},{"targetId":"n0000084","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"Increasing the block size s enlarges the family of doubly stochastic mixing matrices reachable through block-orthogonal parameterizations.","densityScore":0.77,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Connects the asymptotic spanning result to parameterization expressivity."}]},{"targetId":"n0000087","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"Residual stream routing determines how information is redistributed among parallel streams between successive residual blocks.","densityScore":0.69,"relation":"enables","reason":"Supplies the machine-learning role of the construction."}]},{"targetId":"n0000090","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"The stream axis d indexes parallel residual representations, while B, L, and D conventionally index batch elements, sequence positions, and feature dimensions.","densityScore":0.74,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains the state tensor dimensions needed to read the recursion."}]},{"targetId":"n0000100","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Squaring the entries of an orthogonal matrix makes every output entry nonnegative while orthogonality makes every row and column sum to one.","densityScore":0.84,"relation":"supports","reason":"Provides the mechanism by which orthostochastic matrices become doubly stochastic."}]},{"targetId":"n0000103","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"The squared Frobenius norm aggregates the nonnegative energy carried from input stream j to output stream i across the s-by-s block.","densityScore":0.82,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Interprets each generalized orthostochastic entry as stream-to-stream mixing mass."}]},{"targetId":"n0000109","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"A mixing map parameterizes residual routing by first constructing an orthogonal block matrix and then converting its block energies into nonnegative stream weights.","densityScore":0.77,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains the role of the learned-parameter-to-matrix map."}]},{"targetId":"n0000112","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"The convergence claim is asymptotic: a fixed finite block size can remain unable to represent some doubly stochastic matrices exactly.","densityScore":0.8,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Distinguishes eventual coverage from finite-s expressivity."}]},{"targetId":"n0000116","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"Permutation matrices are the vertices of the Birkhoff polytope and represent deterministic one-to-one routing between streams.","densityScore":0.75,"relation":"enables","reason":"Explains why meeting permutation vertices matters geometrically."}]},{"targetId":"n0000117","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"Monotonically increasing volume means that enlarging s does not reduce the represented region and can only add accessible doubly stochastic transformations.","densityScore":0.79,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Translates the volume statement into an expressivity guarantee."}]},{"targetId":"n0000121","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"The Karpelevič region is the set of complex eigenvalues attainable by stochastic matrices of a fixed dimension.","densityScore":0.76,"relation":"enables","reason":"Defines the spectral object shown by the figure."}]},{"targetId":"n0000123","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"For d greater than two, the affine constraint of unit row and column sums leaves a multidimensional space rather than a single line segment.","densityScore":0.66,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains the exceptional geometry of the two-stream case."}]},{"targetId":"n0000124","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Embedding a lower-dimensional stochastic matrix as a block within a larger matrix preserves its existing eigenvalues, which accounts for recursive spectral inclusion.","densityScore":0.73,"relation":"supports","reason":"Provides intuition for the union with the lower-dimensional spectrum."}]},{"targetId":"n0000130","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"A skew-symmetric matrix has zero diagonal and independent parameters only above one diagonal, giving n(n-1)/2 degrees of freedom for n=ds.","densityScore":0.82,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains the stated parameter-vector dimension."}]},{"targetId":"n0000131","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"For a skew-symmetric A with I-A invertible, the Cayley transform Q=(I-A)^{-1}(I+A) produces an orthogonal matrix.","densityScore":0.86,"relation":"supports","reason":"States the algebraic route from unconstrained parameters to an orthogonal matrix."}]},{"targetId":"n0000132","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"Because the block-energy map preserves nonnegativity and unit row and column sums, its output can be used directly as a valid residual mixing matrix.","densityScore":0.78,"relation":"supports","reason":"Links exact doubly stochasticity to valid stream routing."}]},{"targetId":"n0000135","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Blockwise squared Frobenius norms are unchanged if an orthogonal block matrix is reflected, so the map identifies determinant-plus-one and determinant-minus-one preimages with the same output.","densityScore":0.88,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains why restricting the preimage to special orthogonal matrices does not lose output coverage."}]},{"targetId":"n0000136","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"Path-connected parameter spaces allow continuous movement between any two valid rotations without crossing a disconnected component of the constraint set.","densityScore":0.77,"relation":"supports","reason":"Explains the optimization benefit of path-connectedness."}]},{"targetId":"n0000139","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"The resulting orthogonal matrix preserves Euclidean norms when applied to vectors, so it does not amplify or attenuate a vector solely through the rotation step.","densityScore":0.81,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Clarifies the relevant norm-preservation property."}]},{"targetId":"n0000140","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Softmax derivatives become small when one logit dominates, whereas an orthogonal parameterization avoids enforcing simplex weights through exponentials and normalization.","densityScore":0.82,"relation":"supports","reason":"Explains the proposed source of faster optimization."}]},{"targetId":"n0000141","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"An ill-conditioned parameter-to-matrix Jacobian can turn a moderate parameter update into either a negligible or disproportionately large change in the mixing matrix.","densityScore":0.77,"relation":"enables","reason":"Defines why Jacobian conditioning matters for gradients."}]},{"targetId":"n0000142","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"Routing near a polytope boundary is nearly deterministic, so moving back toward balanced routing can require leaving a parameter regime with weak local sensitivity.","densityScore":0.8,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains the stated learn-versus-unlearn trade-off."}]},{"targetId":"n0000143","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"Worst-case amplification measures the largest factor by which a product of residual mixing matrices can increase the norm of an input perturbation.","densityScore":0.79,"relation":"enables","reason":"Defines the stability quantity before its use."}]},{"targetId":"n0000146","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"A doubly stochastic matrix has induced one-norm and infinity-norm equal to one, and products of such matrices remain nonexpansive in those norms.","densityScore":0.77,"relation":"supports","reason":"Supplies the stability property underlying the metric claim."}]},{"targetId":"n0000147","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"If A and B each preserve the all-ones vector on both the left and right, then AB also preserves it on both sides and remains nonnegative.","densityScore":0.87,"relation":"supports","reason":"Shows why doubly stochasticity survives composition."}]},{"targetId":"n0000148","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"When a shared stability metric cannot distinguish methods, expressivity and optimization speed become the relevant axes for comparing their parameterizations.","densityScore":0.8,"relation":"supports","reason":"Justifies the change in evaluation criteria."}]},{"targetId":"n0000151","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"Covering the complex part of the spectral region permits oscillatory or cyclic stream dynamics, while the real interval captures purely non-oscillatory modes.","densityScore":0.79,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains the functional meaning of the covered eigenvalues."}]},{"targetId":"n0000154","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"Eigenvalues summarize repeated linear dynamics, so their attainable set is a compact proxy for whether a parameterization can realize diverse routing behaviors.","densityScore":0.78,"relation":"supports","reason":"Justifies spectral reach as an expressivity surrogate."}]},{"targetId":"n0000156","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"An eigenvalue λ describes a nonzero direction v whose transformation by H^res changes only its scale and complex phase.","densityScore":0.76,"relation":"enables","reason":"Defines the equation used in the spectral-reach set."}]},{"targetId":"n0000159","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"A larger spectral reach does not prove that every matrix is representable, but it rules out some restrictions that confine dynamics to a small family of eigenvalue behaviors.","densityScore":0.8,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"States the scope and limitation of the surrogate."}]},{"targetId":"n0000178","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"Sampling random target matrices tests whether an optimization procedure can fit representative points throughout the allowable doubly stochastic set rather than only hand-selected cases.","densityScore":0.75,"relation":"enables","reason":"Explains the purpose of the toy-model experiment."}]},{"targetId":"n0000179","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"Nested spectral boundaries separate eigenvalues newly attainable at a given stream count from those already attainable by a lower-dimensional stochastic matrix.","densityScore":0.76,"relation":"enables","reason":"Explains the significance of the two plotted boundaries."}]},{"targetId":"n0000182","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"A one-dimensional image has zero area in a two-dimensional complex spectral plane, so it cannot cover an open region of complex eigenvalues.","densityScore":0.86,"relation":"supports","reason":"Connects the dimensional restriction to failed complex-region coverage."}]},{"targetId":"n0000183","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"Finite block size can produce a large interior subset while still missing boundary behavior associated with nearly extreme doubly stochastic routings.","densityScore":0.78,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains why substantial finite-s coverage need not be complete."}]},{"targetId":"n0000186","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"Eigenvalues with different magnitudes and phases correspond to different rates of decay and oscillation under repeated application of a mixing matrix.","densityScore":0.82,"relation":"supports","reason":"Links spectral diversity to diverse dynamical behavior."}]},{"targetId":"n0000189","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"Every doubly stochastic matrix is stochastic, so its eigenvalues must lie in the stochastic-matrix spectral region even when the parameterization imposes additional constraints.","densityScore":0.78,"relation":"supports","reason":"Explains the outer spectral constraint."}]},{"targetId":"n0000190","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"A restricted boundary means that some extreme eigenvalue configurations allowed by general doubly stochastic matrices remain unreachable at that block size.","densityScore":0.79,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Relates boundary restriction to missing expressivity."}]},{"targetId":"n0000191","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Larger blocks introduce additional orthogonal degrees of freedom before block energies are aggregated into the d-by-d mixing matrix.","densityScore":0.79,"relation":"supports","reason":"Explains why increasing s can broaden spectral support."}]},{"targetId":"n0000193","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Symmetry ties selected stream-pair coefficients together, reducing the number of independently controllable interactions in the residual mixing matrix.","densityScore":0.8,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains how symmetry prunes mixing terms."}]},{"targetId":"n0000194","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"A sparse parameterized subset has fewer independently variable directions than the full Birkhoff polytope, so it cannot generally realize arbitrary stream routings.","densityScore":0.82,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Connects sparse parameterization to reduced expressivity."}]},{"targetId":"n0000195","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"The eigenvalues of a Kronecker product are pairwise products of the eigenvalues of its factors, so repeated products do not create independent new spectral coordinates.","densityScore":0.86,"relation":"supports","reason":"Provides the algebraic reason for invariant spectral reach."}]},{"targetId":"n0000197","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"A two-by-two doubly stochastic matrix has eigenvalue one from row-sum preservation and one remaining real eigenvalue between minus one and one.","densityScore":0.84,"relation":"supports","reason":"Explains the stated two-eigenvalue form."}]},{"targetId":"n0000198","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"For eigenpairs of A and B, tensoring their eigenvectors yields an eigenvector of A⊗B with eigenvalue equal to the product of the two eigenvalues.","densityScore":0.87,"relation":"supports","reason":"Derives the listed Kronecker-product spectrum."}]},{"targetId":"n0000199","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"If all base eigenvalues are real, multiplication preserves their reality, preventing the product construction from reaching oscillatory modes represented by nonreal eigenvalues.","densityScore":0.84,"relation":"supports","reason":"Connects invariance to the missing broader spectral region."}]},{"targetId":"n0000202","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"A cyclic permutation of length greater than two has nonreal roots of unity as eigenvalues, which cannot arise from a construction restricted to real spectra.","densityScore":0.85,"relation":"supports","reason":"Links the spectral restriction to inability to represent longer cycles."}]},{"targetId":"n0000203","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"Real eigenvalues model non-rotating expansion, contraction, or sign reversal; complex-conjugate eigenvalues are needed for persistent rotational or directed cyclic components.","densityScore":0.82,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains the diffusion-versus-advection interpretation."}]},{"targetId":"n0000223","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"Per-layer parameter complexity determines the memory footprint and optimizer-state cost that scale with network depth.","densityScore":0.72,"relation":"enables","reason":"Explains why parameter-count comparisons matter."}]},{"targetId":"n0000227","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"A lower parameter count is beneficial only when the reduced family still contains the routing behaviors required by the task.","densityScore":0.76,"relation":"supports","reason":"States the relevant trade-off between compactness and expressivity."}]}]}
